index 5342204..06ab5c9 100644
--- a/specs/state.md
+++ b/specs/state.md
@@ -273,6 +273,49 @@ Schema boundary note:
 - Post-v2.0 runtimes reject resume from pre-v2.0 state rather than silently remapping old name-keyed lineage/freshness data.
 - When a resumed run reaches a terminal state, `current_step` must be cleared.
 
+## Checksum-Compatible Resume Projection Integrity
+
+A run whose workflow source still has the same root checksum can carry persisted identities the current program no longer owns (a stale or renamed step id, an impossible loop iteration, a call frame whose caller or import alias changed, two resumable frames for one call). Resume therefore audits every scope it will execute against the projection that owns that scope and fails closed before any state mutation or workflow effect. The state schema stays `"2.1"`; no migration, receipt, or persisted audit state exists, and pre-v2.0 and pre-2.1 reusable-call state remain rejected.
+
+Scope audit and ordering:
+- `orchestrator.workflow.resume_projection_integrity.audit_scope(bundle, state, scope_path)` audits one scope. It reads only the bundle and state it is given (no workflow or bundle load, no checksum, no file read), never mutates the state, and raises `ResumeProjectionIntegrityError` (a `ValueError` carrying `.error`, the diagnostic below). `ResumeScopePath` is the identity-only path from the root workflow through each entered call frame.
+- After schema and root-checksum acceptance, `orchestrator resume` audits the persisted root scope before observability overrides, executor session or process metadata, executor construction, and the execution prologue. On failure it exits 1, prints the reason to standard error, and persists only the failure envelope below. A run that is already `completed` is a no-op and is not audited.
+- A `WorkflowExecutor` that resumes as the structural root (its state manager is not a call-frame manager) rechecks the authoritative checksum (the file recorded in `workflow_file`, through `StateManager.calculate_checksum`) and then audits the root scope immediately before its prologue. A child call-frame executor does not repeat the root guard.
+- A reached call revalidates after the ordinary parent visit/start publication and after authored-input, managed/runtime-input, write-root, checksum, and applicable resume-bound-input validation, but before the selected callee's frame or state is created or changed and before any callee effect. It calls `WorkflowStateProjection.resolve_call_boundary(slot_index, call_step_id)` once for the reached call identity; exactly one current call boundary must own it, and that boundary's current `import_alias` selects the callee bundle (the persisted alias is checked, never used to select). Nested scopes apply the same rule recursively.
+- Code that runs the audit calls it through the module-level name `audit_scope` in `orchestrator.workflow.calls`, `orchestrator.workflow.executor`, and `orchestrator.cli.commands.resume`.
+
+Explicit identities and omission compatibility:
+- Every present explicit durable step identity (`steps.<name>.step_id`, `current_step.step_id`, `call_frames.<id>.call_step_id`) must resolve to exactly one candidate in its owning current scope through `WorkflowStateProjection.enumerate_resume_slots`, `resolve_resume_step_id`, and `resolve_call_boundary`. Qualified loop and call identities resolve only through projection-owned candidates; resume never splits, normalizes, prefix-matches, reconstructs, remaps, or backfills ids and never selects by workflow, module, or procedure name, basename, path suffix, or persisted alias.
+- An absent `step_id` stays compatible for every schema-valid `steps.*` row that the presentation/name/order fallback supports, whatever its status; resume leaves the field absent. `current_step.step_id`, `call_frames.*.call_step_id`, call-frame current position, and loop progress selectors are mandatory.
+
+Loop projection integrity:
+- `for_each` indices are unique non-boolean non-negative integers within `items`; `current_index`, when present, is in range and not already completed.
+- `repeat_until` admits exactly four progress forms: active; terminal condition success; terminal successful exhaustion; terminal failed exhaustion. Active progress has a non-negative `current_iteration` outside `completed_iterations`, contiguous prior history, and consistent condition/result fields. Every terminal form has `current_iteration: null`, keeps the completed-iteration history, and names the terminal evaluated iteration. Success has `last_condition_result: true` and is not exhausted. Successful exhaustion covers the declared maximum range, has `last_condition_result: false`, `exhausted: true`, and (when the loop row is present) a completed loop step. Failed exhaustion covers the declared maximum range, has `last_condition_result: false`, `exhausted` absent or false, and (when the loop row is present) a failed loop step whose `error.type` is `repeat_until_iterations_exhausted`. The declared maximum is the `max_iterations` persisted on the loop frame row.
+- Valid bookkeeping does not excuse a stale or out-of-scope loop-local step id or loop-contained call-boundary id.
+
+Call frames and Workflow Lisp retry lineage:
+- Every call frame validates its caller identity, import alias, boundary ownership, and status (`completed`, `running`, `failed`). Completed frames are unlimited historical records and never resumable. A boundary whose callee is not Workflow Lisp has at most one non-completed frame.
+- A Workflow Lisp boundary is recognised only by the typed capability `provenance.frontend_kind == "workflow_lisp"`. Its non-completed frames form one retry lineage: a base frame `B` (ordinal 0) and retries `B::retry::<n>` (n = 1, 2, ...), with zero or one running member and any number of failed predecessors. Mixed bases, several running members, a duplicate or missing ordinal, a malformed or nested retry marker, an unknown status, and a caller or alias differing from the boundary's fail closed; mapping order never selects a frame. The next fresh retry id is the lowest unused positive ordinal over all members, completed ones included. `index_retry_lineage` and `next_unused_retry_frame_id` implement this.
+- With a running member, its checksum and resume-bound-input validation run first and win on failure; then every failed predecessor is checksum-validated and recursively audited in ordinal order; then the running member's own scope is audited and resumed. Without a running member, every failed predecessor is checksum-validated and recursively audited before the fresh retry frame is allocated.
+- A completed call frame whose id collides with the frame a resumed call would occupy is rejected before any lineage grouping, whatever its persisted `call_step_id`, with the invalid-resume-state contract violation whose context is exactly `{"step", "call", "call_frame_id", "reason": "call_resume_state_invalid", "detail": "completed_call_frame_id_collision"}`; no child state manager or executor is created and the frames are unchanged.
+
+Classic-YAML import aliases are validated before bundle construction: an alias that occurs more than once among the effective `imports` entries (duplicate keys, `<<` merges, repeated top-level `imports` sections, or merge sources each carrying `imports`) is reported once as `imports.<alias>: duplicate import alias` naming each `line L, column C`; repeated top-level `imports` sections with distinct aliases are reported once as `imports: duplicate top-level mapping`. A single merged `imports` section with unique aliases still loads.
+
+Failure and mutation envelopes:
+- The initial CLI root-checksum precheck keeps its behavior (exit 1, nothing written).
+- A structural root executor whose checksum recheck fails persists `error` = `{"type": "workflow_checksum_mismatch", "message": "Workflow has been modified since the run started", "context": {"workflow_file", "persisted_checksum", "current_checksum", "reason"}}` with `reason` one of `workflow_modified`, `missing_recorded_checksum`, `missing_workflow_path`, `workflow_unavailable` (`StateManager.record_workflow_checksum_mismatch`). It changes only root `status`, `error`, and `updated_at`, and stops before the projection audit and prologue.
+- A root projection failure (CLI or executor) changes only root `status`, `error`, and `updated_at` (`StateManager.record_resume_projection_integrity_failure`). A failure caught by the executor's second root audit keeps already-open session/process metadata, stops before the prologue, and the CLI closes the session as failed; a failing session close never replaces the diagnostic.
+- An unchanged `current_step` left by such an envelope (error type `resume_projection_integrity_error` or `workflow_checksum_mismatch`) is forensic: the run is reported failed with its error, the status snapshot shows that step as `pending` (`current_visit_count` null, counted under `pending`), `render_status_markdown` does not call it in progress, and heartbeat and stalled-run interpretation do not treat it as live.
+- A reached-callee failure before the callee is constructed becomes the caller's failed call-step result and the scope/run error; the selected callee frame and state stay untouched; existing ancestor frames persist ordinary failed step/current/visit/frame snapshots and promote the exact same diagnostic, unchanged, to the root. It is never wrapped in `call_failed`.
+- `resume_projection_integrity_error` is a sticky, non-routable terminal failure: authored `success`, `failure`, and `always` routes, `on_error=continue`, and `for_each`/`repeat_until` continuation cannot consume it (`_handle_control_flow` returns `_stop`). Configured finalization may still run, but a finalization failure is supplemental: it is recorded under `finalization` as `status: failed` with `failure` = `{"step", "step_id", "error"}` where `error` is the cleanup's projection error or `null` for an ordinary failure, and it never wraps, replaces, clears, or downgrades the run's projection diagnostic. When the projection error arises only inside finalization, it becomes the run error and `finalization.failure.error`.
+
+Diagnostics and privacy:
+- Every audit failure is `{"type": "resume_projection_integrity_error", "message": "Resume projection integrity audit failed: <reason>", "context": {...}}`. Context keys, always present: `diagnostic_schema` (`"resume_projection_integrity_error.v1"`), `reason`, `scope_path` (`{"kind": "root", "workflow_file"}` then one `{"kind": "call_frame", "frame_id"}` per frame entered), `field` (dotted persisted path or null), `offending_value` (scalar, or null when absent or a container), `expected_owner` (`workflow_file`, `workflow_checksum`, `projection_scope` of `"root"` or `"call_frame"`), `candidate_count` (integer or null), `call_boundary_step_id` (the audited frame's call step id or null).
+- Reasons (`field`, offending value, `candidate_count`): `unknown_explicit_step_id` (`steps.<name>.step_id`, id, 0); `presentation_slot_mismatch` (`steps.<name>.step_id` or `current_step.step_id`, id, 1); `out_of_scope_step_id` (`current_step.step_id` or `call_frames.<id>.call_step_id`, id, 0); `unclaimed_explicit_step_row` (`steps.<name>.step_id`, id, 0); `missing_required_identity` (for example `current_step.step_id`; null, null); `missing_call_boundary` (`call_frames.<id>.call_step_id`, id, 0); `ambiguous_call_boundary` (same field, id, count); `ambiguous_resumable_call_frame` (`call_frames`, number of resumable frames, that number); `persisted_import_alias_mismatch` (`call_frames.<id>.import_alias`, alias, 1); `missing_imported_bundle` (`imports.<alias>`, alias, 1); `unsupported_shape` (a container or value of the wrong shape; null for containers); `invalid_loop_progress` (for example `repeat_until.<loop>.completed_iterations`).
+- Diagnostics never expose bound inputs, context values, artifacts, prompts, provider output, secrets, whole frame state, whole projections, or the contents of a malformed list or mapping used as an identity. The auditor reads no procedure-migration, identity-retirement, or other migration evidence and keeps no receipts or persisted audit state.
+
+Typed Workflow Lisp provenance: every bundle produced by the Workflow Lisp compiler (including imported Workflow Lisp bundles and every lowered bundle) carries `provenance.frontend_kind == "workflow_lisp"`; a classic-YAML bundle passed through `imported_workflow_bundles` keeps `frontend_kind` `None` and is linked as the same object; `build_frontend_bundle` keeps the kind the compiler set.
+
